  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 153 PM EDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\n***** LOW SEVERE STORM RISK SATURDAY, COOLER SUNDAY-MONDAY *****\n\nA stronger trough is forecast to dive south out of Manitoba Friday \nnight, reaching the Lower Great Lakes by Saturday evening and \nbringing a cold front through the local area by Sunday morning. It's \nunclear whether we will see sufficient moisture return ahead of this \nfront to have a severe thunderstorm risk, particularly with the \nminimum in shear along the trough axis during the afternoon. \nHowever, some machine learning guidance is advertising 15+% severe \nprobabilities over portions of the local area. At this time, SPC's \nday 3 outlook clips areas east of I-75 in northeast Kentucky with a \nlevel 1 of 5 (marginal) risk, which is also the area ML guidance is \nhitting the hardest. Still, a couple RRFS iterations have hit areas \nwest of I-65 (where shear is slightly higher under northwest flow) \nwith a separate area of thunderstorms; we'll keep an eye on this \nsignal in the other CAMs once they begin to capture the event.\n\nBehind the front, conditions should turn seasonably dry and slightly \ncooler with afternoon highs in the low 80s both Sunday and Monday, \nand overnight lows possibly (40-70% chance, according to NBM) \ndipping into the 50s in spots both Sunday and Monday nights. \nEventually, however, ridging should expand back into the region from \nthe west, bringing an attendant increase in warmth/humidity and \nstorm chances (20-40%) along its periphery by midweek.",
